MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Francisco,

Here are two ideas:
I would compare the outputs of rsf.select() rather than rsf.fit(). When you have unsupported parameters, they can cause numerical errors in the parameter estimate covariance calculation, which could be the culprit here. This is general to anything using maximum likelihood (LM, GLM, etc.).
If you are taking data only during the day|night when they are active, then the straightforward application of weighting will put too much probability mass on the times before and after inactivity. If weights are needed with these kinds of data, you have to contract the inactive time gaps first.
Best,
Chris

